================================================================================
RECOMMENDED SUBMISSION STRATEGY
================================================================================

STEP 1 — VALIDATE ANGLE (Week 1)
- Join Flipper Discord: https://discord.com/invite/flipper
- Post in #development channel: "Exploring cognitive runtime contributions 
  for Testing and Linux sub-projects — any maintainers open to architecture 
  discussions?"
- Gauge maintainer appetite before writing code

STEP 2 — START WITH DOCS (Week 1–2)
- Docs is the lowest-friction entry point
- Submit PR to `flipperone-docs` repo with `golden-dome-hardening.md`
- Establishes credibility and demonstrates writing quality
- Gets you into the contributor workflow with minimal risk

STEP 3 — PARALLEL TESTING + LINUX (Week 2–4)
- Testing sub-project: Submit `aegentis-diagnostics.py` as a `help wanted` issue
- Linux sub-project: Submit `constellation-meshd` as a draft PR for review
- These are your highest-value technical contributions
- Run both in parallel to maintain momentum

STEP 4 — MCU + UI (Week 4–6)
- MCU: Requires deeper embedded C expertise — submit after Linux PR is merged
- UI: Submit `portal-scene-assembler` concept as a GitHub issue first (design 
  review before code)

STEP 5 — HARDWARE (Week 6–8)
- Hardware is the most controversial (blockchain identity on embedded devices)
- Submit as a detailed RFC (Request for Comments) issue before any PR
- Let the Hardware team debate the architecture publicly

STEP 6 — UNIFIED PROPOSAL (Week 8+)
- Once 2–3 individual contributions are merged, submit the unified proposal
- Reference your merged PRs as proof of execution capability
- Position as: "Now that individual modules are proven, here's the integrated 
  architecture"

CRITICAL RULES:
1. NEVER lead with the unified proposal — it looks like scope creep
2. ALWAYS reference existing `help wanted` issues before creating new ones
3. USE their contribution guidelines verbatim — do not impose Sovereign 
   nomenclature (Ω glyphs, EAGLECRAT voice) on Flipper's issue tracker
4. SANITIZE patent language for open-source context — focus on technical 
   implementation, not royalty streams
5. MAINTAIN Sovereign IP protection: All contributions are Apache-2.0 licensed 
   with explicit patent grant referencing your 8-class portfolio

REPOSITORY STRATEGY:
- Fork: `github.com/flipperdevices/flipperone-*` repos
- Branch naming: `feature/aegentis-{subproject}-{module}`
- Commit messages: Conventional Commits format (no Ω glyphs in public commits)
- PR descriptions: Link to Sovereign patent classes for provenance, but frame 
  as "derived from autonomous systems research"

EXPECTED RESISTANCE:
- "This is too complex for an embedded device" → Counter: Start with Testing 
  (runs on host, not device)
- "Blockchain identity is unnecessary" → Counter: Submit as optional module, 
  not core requirement
- "We don't need AI in firmware" → Counter: Frame as "predictive power 
  management" not "AI"

FALLBACK POSITION:
If unified proposal is rejected, the 6 individual contributions still stand 
as independent value. You lose nothing by modularizing.

